# Helgrind suppressions.
#
# Helgrind reasons about pthread primitives only. It does not model C11
# _Atomic, so a relaxed atomic load/store — which compiles to a plain aligned
# mov, exactly as it should on x86 — is reported as a data race. Each entry
# below is that case and nothing else. Anything else that shows up is a real
# finding: do not extend this file to make helgrind quiet.
#
# (TSan, which does understand _Atomic, is the authority for these; helgrind's
# value is that it runs on a plain, uninstrumented build and catches lock-order
# inversions and genuinely unprotected accesses.)

# src/audio.c g_volume: master volume, written by audioSetMasterVolume from the
# GUI/input side and read by renderAudioBuffer on the audio thread. Declared
# _Atomic float precisely so this is not a race; helgrind cannot see that. The
# aligned 4-byte store/load it emits needs no lock prefix on x86, which is why
# TSan is satisfied and helgrind is not.
#
# Two entries, not one: a helgrind suppression matches the *access site*, and a
# two-sided race has two of them. A single entry naming only the writer
# suppresses the report only while helgrind happens to attribute it that way —
# which flipped between runs as the threads interleaved differently. Name the
# access point on both sides.
{
   g_volume-relaxed-atomic-write
   Helgrind:Race
   fun:audioSetMasterVolume
}
{
   g_volume-relaxed-atomic-read
   Helgrind:Race
   fun:audioGetMasterVolume
}